          "Now, turn to the formalities German law actually does demand, even though it dispenses with consideration entirely. The general rule is Formfreiheit, contracts do not need to be written to be valid. But the BGB carves out specific exceptions requiring notarization, notarielle Beurkundung, before a Notar, most importantly real estate sales under §311b BGB, and certain guarantee or suretyship promises given by a private individual under §766 BGB; other transactions require only simple Schriftform, written form, such as certain lease terms exceeding one year [1]. Failure to meet a required form generally makes the contract void outright, nichtig, not merely unenforceable, and a defect can sometimes be cured only in narrow, statutorily defined circumstances, most notably where a defectively formed land sale is actually completed by the transfer and registration of title, Auflassung and Eintragung, under §311b Abs. 1 Satz 2 BGB, which heals the earlier formal defect retroactively rather than leaving the transaction permanently void. Electronic form, die elektronische Form under §126a BGB, using a qualified electronic signature, can substitute for ordinary Schriftform in many cases, but it cannot substitute for notarization, and a growing share of German legal practice now turns on precisely this distinction between formalities that modern technology can satisfy and the one, notarization, that still requires a physically present Notar.",
