        explanation:
          "Now, turn to what happens once a validly formed, properly formalized contract actually gets performed badly. Since the 2002 Schuldrechtsreform, the reform of the law of obligations that took effect on 1 January 2002, German law organizes all breach scenarios, impossibility, Unmöglichkeit under §275 BGB, delay, Verzug under §286, and defective performance, mangelhafte Leistung, under one single general concept: Pflichtverletzung, breach of duty, the basis for damages claims under §280 BGB [2]. Before the reform, German law instead operated through a patchwork of separate, narrower doctrines, Unmöglichkeit, Verzug, and a judge-made gap-filler called positive Vertragsverletzung, positive breach of contract, that courts had to invent because the original 1900 code simply had no general breach concept at all; the 2002 reform folded all of that into the unified Pflichtverletzung framework that governs the entire rest of this lecture's discussion of remedies [4]. You should treat this unified structure as a distinctly German simplification compared to the separate common-law doctrines you may be used to reaching for depending on exactly which type of breach occurred, and note that Unmöglichkeit itself now comes in several statutory flavors under §275, genuine impossibility, practical impossibility disproportionate to the creditor's interest, and personal impossibility for a service only the debtor could ever have performed, each excusing performance on a slightly different footing.",
