          "We close with the principle that has quietly shaped every concept covered in this lecture, whether or not it was named directly. §242 BGB establishes Treu und Glauben, good faith, not as a narrow, stand-alone doctrine the way a common-law lawyer might expect, but as a pervasive standard that colors the interpretation and performance of every single contract, capable of modifying, and in extreme cases even overriding, an otherwise clear contractual term. German courts also recognize culpa in contrahendo, fault in the negotiation, now codified at §311 Abs. 2 in conjunction with §241 Abs. 2 BGB, which imposes real duties of care and disclosure on parties during contract negotiations, before any contract has actually been formed at all [1]. The doctrine traces back to the Reichsgericht's 1911 Linoleumrollenfall, the linoleum-roll case, in which a shopper was injured by a rolled-up piece of floor covering a shop assistant negligently let fall while no purchase had yet been made, and no contract, and therefore no ordinary contractual duty of care, yet existed between the parties [3]. Treu und Glauben also underlies the doctrine of Wegfall der Geschäftsgrundlage, disappearance of the foundation of the transaction, now codified at §313 BGB, which lets a court adjust or unwind a contract where circumstances both sides genuinely assumed would hold have since changed so fundamentally that holding either party to the original bargain would be plainly unreasonable.",
