        title: "The primacy of EU law and German constitutional identity",
        explanation:
          "We close with the still-evolving question of how everything covered in this lecture actually coexists with a body of law sitting above the German constitutional order itself. As an EU member state, Germany accepts that EU law generally takes primacy over national law, but the Bundesverfassungsgericht has developed the Solange, so long as, line of case law reserving the right to review EU measures against core German constitutional identity, particularly fundamental rights and democratic accountability, if EU-level protection ever became genuinely inadequate. The doctrine's name comes from the court's own 1974 Solange I decision, which initially threatened to review EU law against German fundamental rights so long as the European Community itself lacked a comparably robust rights framework, softened considerably in the 1986 Solange II decision, which found EU-level protection had since become generally adequate and scaled back the court's own willingness to intervene, though never fully abandoning the reserved check altogether [8]. The court has since developed a related but distinct check, Ultra-vires-Kontrolle, reviewing whether an EU institution has genuinely acted within the competences actually conferred on it at all, a separate question from whether EU-level rights protection itself is adequate, and one the court invoked directly in its 2020 ruling on the European Central Bank's bond-buying program.",
        whyItMatters:
          "This creates an ongoing, structurally unresolved tension unique to EU member states: German courts generally defer to EU law's primacy while explicitly reserving a constitutional check they have occasionally actually exercised, a balancing act with no equivalent in a purely domestic legal system like the US. You should read this closing concept as a genuine question mark hanging over every other concept covered in this lecture, since the eternity clause, human dignity, federalism, proportionality, all remain, at least in principle, subject to the Bundesverfassungsgericht's own reserved authority to push back against an EU measure it finds genuinely irreconcilable with Germany's own constitutional identity. You should also trace the Solange line's own trajectory explicitly when advising a client on how likely the reserved check actually is to be exercised in a given case, since the shift from Solange I's initial skepticism to Solange II's considerably greater deference shows a court that intervenes only rarely, not one poised to challenge EU law as a matter of routine.",
