    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where a US lawyer would reach for state action doctrine, a specific level of scrutiny, or a diffuse system of judicial review, ask what German law actually puts in that exact spot instead. The Grundgesetz and its eternity clause set the outer boundaries of what can ever be changed, a permanence built directly against Weimar's own constitutional vulnerabilities, anchored by Menschenwürde as the supreme value everything else is interpreted through via the court's own Objektformel, and Drittwirkung shows how that value reaches beyond the state into ordinary private disputes through an indirect, interpretive channel rather than direct horizontal application. The Bundesverfassungsgericht, its own composition protected by a two-thirds cross-party appointment threshold, is the single institution that enforces all of this, reachable through the citizen-accessible Verfassungsbeschwerde and through the government- and court-initiated Normenkontrolle paths. Parteiverbot is the safeguard the constitutional order builds against its own would-be destroyers, itself softened by the Potentialität requirement. Föderalismus allocates power between federal and state levels within those same boundaries through detailed enumerated lists rather than one broad clause, the proportionality test, one of German law's own most globally exported doctrines, is the actual analytical tool used to check whether government action at any level goes too far, and Verwaltungsakt and the Verwaltungsgerichte are where that test actually gets applied, day in and day out, far more often than in any headline constitutional case. The EU law primacy question, traced from Solange I's initial skepticism to Solange II's greater deference, finally, adds a further, still-evolving layer on top of the entire domestic framework this lecture has covered. As you read any German constitutional or regulatory question from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to reach for a US doctrine, state action, tiered scrutiny, diffuse review, that simply has no real counterpart in this system.",
